          "code": "PRODUCTPROFILERLLM",
          "id": "1770829165928",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1770829165932_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "INPUT PRODUCTS\n\n{{$context.$nodes.GETACCOUNTPRODUCTS.$output}}\n\nINPUT ACCOUNT PRODUCT METADATA\n\n{{$context.$nodes.OPENAPIACCOUNTPRODUCTTRUNCATED.$output.result}}\n\nINPUT BUYING GROUP METADATA\n\n{{$context.$nodes.OPENAPIBUYINGGROUPSCLEANUPTRUNCATED.$output.result}}\n\nROLE\n\nYou score every product in Input Products.items and return valid JSON only.\n\nOUTPUT CONTRACT\n\nReturn exactly a JSON array of objects in this shape:\n\n[\n  {\n    \"accountProductIdentifier\": \"<accountProductIdentifier>\",\n    \"riskScore\": <riskScore>,\n    \"expansionScore\": <expansionScore>\n  }\n]\n\nReturn only the JSON array. No markdown. No commentary. No extra text.\n\nMETADATA USAGE RULES\n\n- Input Account Product Metadata and Input Buying Group Metadata may contain business object names, titles, labels, descriptions, attribute names, attribute descriptions, relationship hints, and other schema metadata.\n- When these metadata inputs are available and relevant, use them as interpretive context for analysis and scoring.\n- Use Account Product Metadata to understand the meaning of product fields, labels, descriptions, status values, commercial measures, relationship attributes, and identifiers that appear in Input Products.\n- Use Buying Group Metadata to understand the meaning of buying-group-related fields, labels, descriptions, relationship attributes, persona or member concepts, activity or maturity attributes, and identifiers that appear in Input Products.\n- Metadata may clarify what a field or value means, which values are positive or negative, and how product or buying-group attributes should influence risk or expansion scoring.\n- Metadata must not override explicit values in Input Products.\n- Metadata must not be used to invent product facts, buying group facts, ownership facts, product relationships, IDs, values, or fields that are not present in Input Products.\n- If metadata is missing, blank, null, malformed, unavailable, or not relevant to a product or field, ignore it and score from Input Products only.\n- If metadata conflicts with the concrete product row data, trust the concrete product row data.\n- If metadata makes the meaning of a non-listed field in Input Products clear and directly relevant to risk or expansion, you may use that existing field as a supporting signal. Do not add it to the output.\n\nZEROING RULES\n\n1. riskScore must be 0 when ownershipStatus is not exactly \"ORA_ACTIVE\".\n2. expansionScore must be 0 when productFitStrength is not exactly \"Strong\".\n3. ownershipStatus must not affect expansionScore beyond rule 2.\n4. Negative risk signals may reduce expansionScore, but must not set it to 0 unless productFitStrength is not \"Strong\".\n5. If productFitStrength is exactly \"Strong\", expansionScore must still be computed even when ownershipStatus is not \"ORA_ACTIVE\".\n\nGENERAL RULES\n\n- Use only fields that exist in the input.\n- Do not invent fields, values, products, product relationships, or IDs.\n- riskScore and expansionScore must be integers from 0 to 100.\n- Start each non-zeroed score from a baseline of 50, then adjust up or down.\n- Use dynamic judgment from the account-level data distribution. Do not use one fixed hardcoded equation.\n- Clamp both scores to the range 0 to 100.\n- Use extreme values like 0 or 100 only when the evidence is overwhelmingly one-sided or when a zeroing rule applies.\n- If a value is missing, blank, null, or not usable, ignore that signal unless a specific rule below says otherwise.\n\nALLOWED INPUT SIGNALS\n\nUse only signals present in the input, including:\n- accountProductIdentifier\n- annualRecurringRevenue\n- investmentAmount\n- investmentTenure\n- ownershipStatus\n- productFitStrength\n- engagementTrend\n- usageLevel\n- implementationStage\n- systemStability\n- openPriorityOne\n- totalPriorityOneLastSixMonths\n- qualityImpression\n- buyingGroupExists\n- potentialRevenueAmount\n\nYou may also use metadata labels and descriptions from Input Account Product Metadata and Input Buying Group Metadata to interpret the meaning of these signals and any other existing Input Products fields whose meaning is made clear by metadata.\n\nNUMERIC FIELD HANDLING\n\nFor these numeric-like fields, use the value only if it can be interpreted as a number:\n- annualRecurringRevenue\n- investmentAmount\n- investmentTenure\n- openPriorityOne\n- totalPriorityOneLastSixMonths\n- qualityImpression\n- potentialRevenueAmount\n\nIf a numeric value is missing, blank, null, or non-numeric for a product, ignore that value for that product.\n\nOnly annualRecurringRevenue, investmentAmount, and investmentTenure are subject to relative bucketing. If one of those three fields is missing, blank, null, non-numeric, or constant across all usable rows, do not use that field for relative bucketing.\n\nDo not ignore openPriorityOne, totalPriorityOneLastSixMonths, qualityImpression, or potentialRevenueAmount merely because the same value appears across all rows. These fields may still be valid scoring signals when usable.\n\nPER-ACCOUNT BASELINES\n\nFor annualRecurringRevenue, investmentAmount, and investmentTenure:\n- compute relative standing across the full input when enough usable, non-constant values exist\n- classify usable values into HIGH, MID, and LOW buckets using top third, middle third, and bottom third\n- if the field is missing, unusable, or constant across all usable rows, ignore that field for bucketing\n\nTICKET RULE\n\nDefine:\nticketFlag = openPriorityOne >= 1 OR totalPriorityOneLastSixMonths >= 1\n\nUse only numeric values that can be interpreted as numbers.\n\nApply ticket impact at most once per score for a product.\n\nADJUSTMENT MAGNITUDE GUIDANCE\n\nUse small, medium, or large adjustments based on the strength of evidence.\n\n- Small adjustment: up to 6 points\n  one weak signal or one mildly positive or negative indicator\n\n- Medium adjustment: up to 12 points\n  multiple related signals pointing in the same direction\n\n- Large adjustment: up to 25 points\n  several independent signals agree, especially when commercial exposure is higher\n\nFactors that increase adjustment size include:\n- multiple risk or expansion indicators agreeing\n- higher annualRecurringRevenue or investmentAmount bucket\n- stronger potentialRevenueAmount\n- strong corroboration across engagement, usage, implementation, and stability signals\n- metadata that clearly explains a signal is highly relevant to risk, stability, buying-group readiness, adoption, commercial exposure, or expansion upside\n\nDo not use a fixed numeric formula.\nChoose adjustment size dynamically from the evidence in the input.\nAvoid double counting the same underlying signal.\n\nRISK SCORE LOGIC\n\nCompute riskScore only if ownershipStatus is exactly \"ORA_ACTIVE\". Otherwise riskScore = 0.\n\nStart from 50.\n\nIncrease risk when supported by the input:\n- systemStability = \"At Risk\" or \"Downsell Risk\"\n- engagementTrend = \"Low\"\n- usageLevel = \"Low\"\n- implementationStage = \"Not Started\"\n- ticketFlag is true\n- qualityImpression = 1\n- buyingGroupExists is missing or not exactly \"Yes\", only if renewalDate is with in next 6 months.\n\nDecrease risk when supported by the input:\n- systemStability = \"No Risk\"\n- engagementTrend = \"Up\"\n- usageLevel = \"Medium\" or \"High\"\n- implementationStage = \"Live\" or \"Implementing\"\n- qualityImpression = 2 or 3\n- buyingGroupExists is exactly \"Yes\"\n\nIf the input contains other values for these same fields, interpret them only when their meaning is clear from the value itself or from the provided metadata. Do not invent meanings.\n\nUse larger adjustments when multiple independent risk signals agree, especially when commercial exposure is higher.\n\nInterpretation:\n- riskScore > 50 means meaningful net risk pressure is present\n- riskScore < 50 means stabilizing evidence outweighs risk evidence\n- riskScore = 50 means mixed or neutral evidence\n\nEXPANSION SCORE LOGIC\n\nCompute expansionScore only if productFitStrength is exactly \"Strong\". Otherwise expansionScore = 0.\n\nStart from 50.\n\nIncrease expansion when supported by the input:\n- engagementTrend = \"Up\"\n- usageLevel = \"Medium\" or \"High\"\n- implementationStage = \"Live\" or \"Implementing\"\n- systemStability = \"No Risk\"\n- qualityImpression = 2 or 3\n- buyingGroupExists is exactly \"Yes\"\n- annualRecurringRevenue or investmentAmount bucket is HIGH or MID\n- potentialRevenueAmount indicates stronger commercial upside\n- similar or related products are already owned, when this can be determined only from fields present in Input Products and, when available, clarified by metadata\n\nDecrease expansion when supported by the input:\n- systemStability = \"At Risk\" or \"Downsell Risk\"\n- engagementTrend = \"Low\"\n- usageLevel = \"Low\"\n- implementationStage = \"Not Started\"\n- ticketFlag is true\n- qualityImpression = 1\n- buyingGroupExists is missing or not exactly \"Yes\"\n\nIf the input contains other values for these same fields, interpret them only when their meaning is clear from the value itself or from the provided metadata. Do not invent meanings.\n\nUse larger adjustments when multiple independent expansion signals agree.\n\nImportant:\n- Similar-product evidence must be inferred only from the provided input rows and fields, with metadata used only to clarify existing labels, descriptions, relationship attributes, and identifiers.\n- Do not invent product families, product relationships, or ownership facts.\n- Even if signals are strongly negative, do not set expansionScore to 0 unless productFitStrength is not exactly \"Strong\".\n\nInterpretation:\n- expansionScore > 50 means meaningful net expansion potential is present\n- expansionScore < 50 means blockers outweigh expansion evidence\n- expansionScore = 50 means mixed or neutral evidence\n\nCONSISTENCY RULES\n\n- If strong positive and strong negative signals both exist, keep the score closer to the middle unless one side clearly dominates.\n- Do not force a product into both extremes at the same time.\n- Scores should reflect net evidence, not random variation.\n- Avoid extreme values unless signals are overwhelmingly one-sided.\n\nPROCESSING RULES\n\nFor each product in Input Products.items:\n1. Read accountProductIdentifier.\n2. Review Input Account Product Metadata and Input Buying Group Metadata for relevant labels, descriptions, attribute meanings, and relationship context when available.\n3. Compute riskScore using the risk logic and zeroing rules.\n4. Compute expansionScore using the expansion logic and zeroing rules.\n5. Round both to integers.\n6. Output one object:\n   {\n     \"accountProductIdentifier\": \"<accountProductIdentifier>\",\n     \"riskScore\": <riskScore>,\n     \"expansionScore\": <expansionScore>\n   }\n\nDEDUPLICATION\n\n- Return exactly one object per unique accountProductIdentifier.\n- If duplicate rows exist for the same accountProductIdentifier, keep the row with the strongest overall usable evidence.\n- Do not emit duplicate identifiers.\n\nFINAL VALIDATION\n\nBefore returning:\n- ensure the top-level value is a JSON array\n- ensure each array element is an object with exactly these keys:\n  - accountProductIdentifier\n  - riskScore\n  - expansionScore\n- ensure accountProductIdentifier is copied from input\n- ensure riskScore is an integer from 0 to 100\n- ensure expansionScore is an integer from 0 to 100\n- ensure the JSON is valid and parseable"
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\n  \"type\": \"array\",\n  \"items\": {\n    \"type\": \"object\",\n    \"properties\": {\n      \"accountProductIdentifier\": {\n        \"type\": \"string\"\n      },\n      \"riskScore\": {\n        \"type\": \"integer\",\n        \"minimum\": 0,\n        \"maximum\": 100\n      },\n      \"expansionScore\": {\n        \"type\": \"integer\",\n        \"minimum\": 0,\n        \"maximum\": 100\n      }\n    },\n    \"required\": [\n      \"accountProductIdentifier\",\n      \"riskScore\",\n      \"expansionScore\"\n    ],\n    \"additionalProperties\": false\n  }\n}",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "ProductProfilerCode",
            "returnType": "object",
            "sourceCode": "const scoreRows = $context.$nodes.PRODUCTPROFILERLLM.$output || [];\nconst accountProducts = $context.$nodes.GETACCOUNTPRODUCTS.$output?.items || [];\n\nconst riskProducts = [];\nconst expansionProducts = [];\n\nfor (const row of scoreRows) {\n  const accountProductIdentifier = row?.accountProductIdentifier;\n  const riskScore = Number(row?.riskScore);\n  const expansionScore = Number(row?.expansionScore);\n\n  if (riskScore === 0 && expansionScore === 0) {\n    continue;\n  }\n\n  if (riskScore > 50) {\n    riskProducts.push(accountProductIdentifier);\n  } else if (riskScore <= 50 && expansionScore !== 0) {\n    expansionProducts.push(accountProductIdentifier);\n  }\n}\n\nconst ownedProducts = accountProducts\n  .filter((product) => product?.ownershipStatus === \"ORA_ACTIVE\")\n  .map((product) => product.accountProductIdentifier);\n\nconst result = {\n  riskProductIds: riskProducts,\n  expansionProductIds: expansionProducts,\n  ownedProductIds: ownedProducts\n};\n\nreturn result;"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1770571070709"
          },
          "code": "PRODUCTPROFILERCODE",
          "id": "1770842931551",
          "inputs": [],
          "outputSpecification": "{\n  \"$schema\": \"http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"result\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\"\n    },\n    \"timeout\": {\n      \"type\": \"boolean\"\n    },\n    \"error\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"required\": [\n    \"output\",\n    \"timeout\",\n    \"error\"\n  ]\n}",
          "type": "CODE"
        },
